[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66036,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ewing sarcoma is a small round cell sarcoma showing gene fusions involving a member of the FET family of genes (usually <em>EWSR1</em>) and a member of the ETS family of transcription factors.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66037,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9364/3 Ewing sarcoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66038,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2B52 &amp; XH8KJ8 Ewing sarcoma, primary site &amp; Peripheral neuroectodermal tumour (includes Ewing sarcoma)</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66039,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> peripheral-type primitive neuroectodermal tumour</p>\r\n<p>Some small round cell sarcomas previously considered subtypes of Ewing sarcoma (Ewing-like sarcomas) are genetically and clinically distinct entities, including <em>CIC</em>-fused and <em>BCOR</em>-rearranged sarcomas, which are described in detail in the fifth edition of the soft tissue and bone tumours volume [[WHO Classification of Tumours Editorial Board. Soft tissue and bone tumours. Lyon (France): International Agency for Research on Cancer; 2020. (WHO classification of tumours series, 5th ed.; vol. 3). https://publications.iarc.fr/588.]].</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66040,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66041,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cervix, vagina, and vulva</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66042,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A cervical, vaginal, or vulvar mass is the most common presentation in the female genital tract {10782405; 17557870; 11468565}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66043,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are extremely rare lesions that involve vulva {18602673; 19820381; 28296680; 10782405; 11468565; 17557870; 31108450; 26275259} more frequently than vagina {22007080; 20233457; 10782405; 17557870; 31108450; 24293381} and cervix {22365564; 10664164; 9475198; 31108450}. They affect premenopausal women, with a median age of 29&nbsp;years (range: 10&ndash;65&nbsp;years) {28296680}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66044,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Rare predisposing germline mutations have been reported in paediatric patients {26580448}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66045,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most tumours harbour t(11;22)(q24;q12) {6713356; 6713357}, resulting in <em>EWSR1</em> (<em>EWS</em>)-<em>FLI1</em> fusion {10197607}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66046,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There is a soft, circumscribed, unencapsulated mass with a uniform and/or nodular lobulated cut surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66047,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours consist of a monotonous population of small round cells arranged in sheets or solid aggregates, separated by fibrous septa. Focal rosettes are sometimes present. Cells have round nuclei, coarse chromatin, small nucleoli, and scant to abundant eosinophilic or clear cytoplasm. Mitotic activity is brisk and necrosis common. Immunohistochemically, there is diffuse, strong, membranous CD99 expression and nuclear FLI1 expression. Focal positivity with broad-spectrum cytokeratins is common. Desmin, myogenin, and MYOD1 are negative.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66048,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Aspirates are cellular, composed of a monotonous population of small cells with hyperchromatic nuclei and scant cytoplasm.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66049,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>EWSR1</em> (<em>EWS</em>)-<em>FLI1</em> fusion is detected in most tumours, although other variants can also be encountered.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66050,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> small cell morphology; membranous CD99 staining; demonstration of <em>EWSR1</em>-<em>FLI1</em> or variant fusion (where available).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66051,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Staging is the same as for other soft tissue neoplasms at the corresponding site.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 66052,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 447,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Prognostic factors include stage at presentation, tumour location, tumour size, patient age, and response to therapy. In molecularly confirmed cases, most patients have no evidence of disease after complete surgical resection {17557870; 28296680}. Primary Ewing sarcoma of the vulvar skin may have a better prognosis {19783720; 22098102}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:37:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]